WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.301 --> 00:00:10.966
That day I wanted to see Mr. Rochester, but I feared seeing him too. I wanted to hear his voice again, yet I feared meeting his eye.

2
00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:14.793
During the morning I was sure he would visit the schoolroom.

3
00:00:14.794 --> 00:00:21.610
But the morning passed as usual, and no one arrived to interrupt the quiet course of Adele's studies.

4
00:00:21.611 --> 00:00:26.948
At one point I heard some bustle in the vicinity of Mr. Rochester's bedroom.

5
00:00:26.949 --> 00:00:32.202
"What a blessing that our master was not burned in his bed!" exclaimed Leah.

6
00:00:32.203 --> 00:00:39.003
"How fortunate that he had the presence of mind to think of the water jug!" said Mrs. Fairfax.

7
00:00:39.004 --> 00:00:42.916
Then I heard the sound of scrubbing and putting things in order.

8
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:50.583
Later when I passed the room as I went to lunch, I saw that everything was restored to its proper place.

9
00:00:50.584 --> 00:00:55.097
Leah stood on the window seat, cleaning soot from the window.

10
00:00:55.098 --> 00:01:00.811
I was about to ask her what had happened because I wanted to hear what the servants had been told.

11
00:01:00.812 --> 00:01:06.000
But as I entered the room, I suddenly noticed a second woman.

12
00:01:06.001 --> 00:01:11.582
Grace Poole was sitting in a chair, sewing new curtains for the bed

13
00:01:11.582 --> 00:01:20.480
She was intent on her work, and her face showed none of the desperation I'd expect from someone who'd just tried to kill her master.

14
00:01:20.481 --> 00:01:32.401
I was amazed. Hadn't Mr. Rochester immediately gone up to the third floor to confront her and accuse her of attempted murder? And yet she sat here so coolly.

15
00:01:32.402 --> 00:01:42.988
She looked up while I still gazed at her. "Good morning, miss," she said in her usual brief way and went on sewing. I decided to test her.

16
00:01:42.989 --> 00:01:46.730
"Good morning, Grace," I replied.

17
00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:52.897
"Has anything happened here? I thought I heard the servants talking together a while ago."

18
00:01:52.898 --> 00:02:00.048
"Master was reading in bed last night. He fell asleep with his candle lit, and the bed curtains caught on fire.

19
00:02:00.049 --> 00:02:04.714
Fortunately he awoke in time and put out the flames with the water in his pitcher."

20
00:02:04.715 --> 00:02:11.708
I stared at her. "Did Mr. Rochester wake no one? Did nobody hear him?"

21
00:02:11.709 --> 00:02:19.027
Grace raised her eyes. "The servants would be unlikely to hear because, as you know, their rooms are far away.

22
00:02:19.028 --> 00:02:25.534
Mrs. Fairfax's room and yours are the nearest to master's, but she's old and a heavy sleeper."

23
00:02:25.535 --> 00:02:36.407
She paused and said in a significant tone, "But you are young, miss, and I assume a light sleeper. Perhaps you heard something?"

24
00:02:36.408 --> 00:02:43.914
I lowered my voice so Leah couldn't hear me. "I'm certain I heard a strange laugh."

25
00:02:43.915 --> 00:02:52.858
Grace carefully threaded her needle. "It's unlikely master would laugh when he was in such danger. You must have been dreaming."

26
00:02:52.859 --> 00:02:56.398
"I was not dreaming!" I said indignantly.

27
00:02:56.399 --> 00:03:00.589
"You didn't think of opening the door and looking out into the corridor?"

28
00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:06.581
She seemed to be cross-examining me, trying to trick me into giving her some information.

29
00:03:06.582 --> 00:03:12.307
I was afraid if she discovered that I knew what she'd done, she would try to harm me.

30
00:03:12.308 --> 00:03:15.552
"On the contrary, I locked my door."

31
00:03:15.553 --> 00:03:26.896
"That was a wise thing to do," she said. "I've never heard of thieves attempting to rob Thornfield, but everyone knows there are many valuables and very few servants here."

32
00:03:26.897 --> 00:03:31.718
We were interrupted by Mary, the cook, telling us that lunch was ready.

33
00:03:31.719 --> 00:03:38.218
Grace said that she would take hers upstairs, but I went down to join Mrs. Fairfax.

34
00:03:38.219 --> 00:03:42.402
I ate hardly anything because my mind was full of questions.

35
00:03:42.403 --> 00:03:45.941
Why had Mr. Rochester sworn me to secrecy?

36
00:03:45.941 --> 00:03:55.956
Why didn't he have Grace arrested for trying to kill him? What power did she hold over him? Had he once been romantically involved with her?

37
00:03:55.957 --> 00:04:03.101
But she was so plain. And yet, I reminded myself, I was plain and he seemed to favor me.

38
00:04:03.102 --> 00:04:07.260
I remembered his voice and the way he looked at me the night before.

39
00:04:07.261 --> 00:04:13.530
I passed the afternoon with Adele in the schoolroom, and I still expected to see Mr. Rochester.

40
00:04:13.531 --> 00:04:22.424
Our school day ended, and I wanted to talk to Mr. Rochester more than ever. I needed to ask him again about Grace Poole.

41
00:04:22.425 --> 00:04:31.434
I knew the pleasure of alternately vexing and soothing him; it was one I delighted in and I was eager to engage in it now.

42
00:04:31.435 --> 00:04:39.806
At last someone knocked at my door. But it was only Leah, telling me that tea was ready in Mrs. Fairfax's room.

43
00:04:39.807 --> 00:04:49.251
"You must want your tea," said Mrs. Fairfax as she poured me a cup. "You ate hardly anything at lunch, and you look flushed."

44
00:04:49.252 --> 00:04:54.463
"Oh, I'm quite well!" I protested. "I never felt better."

45
00:04:54.464 --> 00:05:01.883
Mrs. Fairfax looked out her window. "Mr. Rochester has had a favorable day for his journey."

46
00:05:01.884 --> 00:05:05.324
"Journey? I didn't know he was out."

47
00:05:05.325 --> 00:05:17.209
"Oh, yes." Mrs. Fairfax sat down at the table. "He set off first thing this morning for Mr. Eshton's place ten miles on the other side of Millcote.

48
00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:23.891
I believe there's quite a group assembled there. He's likely to stay a week or more."

49
00:05:23.892 --> 00:05:27.785
"Are there ladies at Mr. Eshton's?" I blurted out.

50
00:05:27.786 --> 00:05:36.273
"Mrs. Eshton and her daughters, very elegant ladies, and then there are Blanche and Mary Ingram, most beautiful women indeed.

51
00:05:36.274 --> 00:05:43.201
I last saw Blanche when she was 18 and came to a Christmas party here half a dozen years ago."

52
00:05:43.202 --> 00:05:47.376
"And what was she like?" I put down my teacup.

53
00:05:47.377 --> 00:05:53.854
Mrs. Fairfax went on to describe a tall and graceful young lady with black hair and eyes.

54
00:05:53.855 --> 00:06:04.350
"All the gentlemen admired her not only for her beauty but for her accomplishments. I remember that she and Mr. Rochester sang a duet.

55
00:06:04.351 --> 00:06:10.927
And when she sat down at the piano, Mr. Rochester said that she played remarkably well."

56
00:06:10.928 --> 00:06:21.518
I remembered his comments on my own musical talent. As I finished my tea, I wondered aloud if Mr. Rochester and Miss Ingram would marry.

57
00:06:21.519 --> 00:06:26.586
But Mrs. Fairfax thought it unlikely given the difference in their ages.

58
00:06:26.587 --> 00:06:39.155
After tea I retired to my room. "Jane, you're a fool," I said to myself. "You are not a favorite of Mr. Rochester's. Open your eyes!

59
00:06:39.156 --> 00:06:45.473
It does no woman any good to be flattered by a superior who has no intention of marrying her."

60
00:06:45.474 --> 00:06:54.957
I sentenced myself to draw a self-portrait in chalk and to paint a fine likeness of Blanche, based on Mrs. Fairfax's description.

61
00:06:54.958 --> 00:07:02.538
My picture, which I labeled "Portrait of a poor, plain governess," took me only two hours the next day.

62
00:07:02.539 --> 00:07:09.950
I worked for days on the other portrait, which I titled "Blanche, an accomplished lady of rank."

63
00:07:09.951 --> 00:07:18.958
I was grateful for this exercise because without it I would have been unable to tolerate what happened next.

